import pf.Html
import ../../../Demos/GuardLimits
import ../../../Demos/GuardPlayground
import ../../../Demos/GuardClients
import ../../../Demos/GuardFlags
import ../../../Demos/GuardCheckout
import ../../../Demos/GuardHeaders
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Note
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Ui/Demo

## The guard package's page: rate limits, feature flags and small request guards, as middleware.
## Its tables are the package's own answers, worked out when the site is built; one demo runs a
## rate limit in the browser, with a clock moved by hand.
meta = {
	title: "Guard",
	description: "Rate limits, feature flags and request guards for Roc Solid apps, as middleware, decided by plain Roc that a test runs with a fake clock.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "install", label: "Installing it" },
		{ id: "first", label: "A first guard" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Rate limits" },
		{ id: "try", label: "Try a limit" },
		{ id: "proxies", label: "Behind a proxy" },
		{ id: "flags", label: "Feature flags" },
		{ id: "guards", label: "Other guards" },
		{ id: "modules", label: "The modules" },
		{ id: "status", label: "Tested, and limits" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/guard</code>]}
				title="Rate limits, feature flags and guards, as middleware"
			>
				Guard protects an app's routes. A login form takes 5 tries a minute from each address, a new
				checkout goes to staff first and then to a quarter of everyone, every answer carries security
				headers, and an admin can put the whole site into maintenance. Each guard is
				<a href={"${Paths.routing}#middleware"}>middleware</a>, and what it decides is plain Roc, so the
				tables on this page are the package's own answers, worked out when the site was built.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="install">Installing it</Heading>
			<p>
				Name the package in your app's header, <code>main.roc</code>:
				<code>{"guard: \"../packages/guard/main.roc\""}</code>. Its middleware uses the server platform,
				so your app must be on it; the browser's half of the app sees only the modules that need no
				platform (the logic, and the <code>{"<Flagged>"}</code> component).
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>In the server's modules (your middleware, your endpoints): <code>import guard.Guard</code>, and <code>guard.FlagStore</code> for flags.</li>
				<li>In a <code>.rocx</code> file: <code>import guard.Flagged</code>, which <code>rocx build</code> copies into the app.</li>
				<li>
					Optional: to keep feature flags' overrides across a restart, an app with a database lists the
					package's <a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#plugins"}>plugin</a>,
					<code>{"plugins: [FlagStore.plugin({})]"}</code> (<a href="#flags">feature flags</a>).
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="first">A first guard</Heading>
			<p>
				The app's default middleware runs around every request, and a route file can name its own, which
				runs around that route alone. Here every answer gets security headers, a body over a megabyte is
				refused, the site can be put into maintenance, everything under <code>/api</code> is limited for
				each user, and the login route is limited for each address:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_middlewares.file} source={Sources.guard_middlewares.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.guard_route.file} source={Sources.guard_route.rocx} />
			<p>
				The sixth try in a minute is answered <code>429 Too Many Requests</code>, with
				<code>Retry-After</code> and the <code>RateLimit-*</code> headers. Every guard hands on the
				context it is given, unchanged, so you can put guards anywhere in a chain.
			</p>
			<p>
				This code is from <code>packages/guard/example/</code>, a whole app that builds and runs, and
				that its tests ask over HTTP.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Rate limits</Heading>
			<p>
				A limit says how many requests a client may send, and in how long.
				<code>RateLimit.per_minute(5)</code> is a token bucket: a client may send 5 at once, then one
				every 12 seconds. <code>.sliding()</code> counts the same limit in a sliding window instead: never
				more than 5 in any minute. Both are plain functions. A client's state and the time go in, and
				whether the request may go on, with the client's next state, comes out.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_limits.file} source={Sources.guard_limits.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Here are the same eleven requests under each limit, as <code>RateLimit.check</code> answered them
				when this page was built. The clock is a list of seconds, as it is in the package's tests.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<GuardLimits />
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				The bucket lets the first five through, refuses the sixth, and gives a token back every 12
				seconds. A refused request costs nothing, so hammering doesn't push the next allowed one back.
				The sliding window refuses everything after the fifth until the five weigh less. Its count is this
				minute's requests plus last minute's, weighted by how much of last minute the window still
				covers. So it keeps two numbers for each client, however high the limit.
			</p>

			<Heading id="try">Try a limit</Heading>
			<p>
				The same <code>RateLimit.check</code>, in your browser, with a clock you move by hand. Nothing
				is sent anywhere: each line is what the server's middleware would answer.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo>
			<div data-demo="guard-limit"><GuardPlayground /></div>
		</Demo>
		<NoHydration>
			<details>
				<summary>The demo: <code>{Sources.guard_playground.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.guard_playground.file} source={Sources.guard_playground.rocx} />
			</details>

			<h3>Who is counted</h3>
			<p>
				<code>Guard.rate_limit(name, limit, key)</code> counts each key separately. The
				<code>name</code> keeps one limit's counts apart from another's (<code>"login"</code>,
				<code>"api"</code>). The key says who a request counts as:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Counts</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Guard.ip(trust)</code></td><td>each address, found as <a href="#proxies">trust</a> says; an IPv6 address by its first 64 bits, the block one household is given</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Guard.user(|context| context.user, trust)"}</code></td><td>each signed-in user, as the context names them; each address when signed out</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Guard.route</code></td><td>each method and path</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Guard.both(key, key)</code></td><td>each pair: <code>Guard.both(Guard.ip(trust), Guard.route)</code> is each address on each route</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"|request, context| …"}</code></td><td>whatever your function says. A request whose key is <code>""</code> isn't limited</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>

			<h3>What a client is told</h3>
			<p>
				An allowed request's answer says where the client stands: <code>RateLimit-Limit</code>,
				<code>RateLimit-Remaining</code>, <code>RateLimit-Reset</code> (seconds until the whole limit is
				back) and <code>RateLimit-Policy</code> (<code>5;w=60</code>: 5 in 60 seconds). A refused one is
				a <code>429</code> with those headers, <code>Retry-After</code> (exactly how many seconds until
				one more would be allowed), and a line of text. A form posted without the runtime is refused the
				way an endpoint refuses one: it goes back to its page with what was typed and a flash of the
				<code>429</code>, so the form shows it where it shows any refusal.
			</p>

			<h3>Memory stays bounded</h3>
			<p>
				Each limit keeps at most 10,000 clients (<code>Guard.rate_limit_with</code> takes another number).
				A client whose limit is whole again is forgotten first, which changes nothing. Past the limit, the
				client seen longest ago is forgotten, and starts again. Each limit's clients are split into 64
				tallies, so a request reads and writes about 150 of them at most.
			</p>
			<p>
				The counts live in the server's memory, in <a href={Paths.platform_kv}>Kv</a>, never in the
				database. A request reads its tally and swaps the next one in; when another request changed it
				first, it reads again, so no hit is lost. If a tally can't be changed after many tries, the
				request goes on and the log says why: a limiter that fails shouldn't take the site down. A restart
				starts every limit again.
			</p>

			<Heading id="proxies">Behind a proxy</Heading>
			<p>
				Most apps are served behind a proxy, which connects to the server for the client and adds the
				address it saw to <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>. A client can write anything it likes in that
				header too, so the header is read only as far as the app says its own proxies wrote it. Take one
				request: it came from 10.0.0.2, the app's proxy, and its <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> is
				<code>6.6.6.6, 198.51.100.4</code>. The client wrote the first address itself; the proxy added
				the second, the address it saw. Here is who the client is, by each way to trust, as
				<code>ClientIp.of</code> worked it out when this page was built:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<GuardClients />
			</TableWrap>
			<ul>
				<li><code>Direct</code> takes the address the connection came from, and ignores the headers. Use it when nothing is in front of the server.</li>
				<li><code>Hops(1)</code> says one proxy is in front, and finds the address it saw. <code>Hops(2)</code> claims two, so here it trusts what the client wrote.</li>
				<li>
					<code>Proxies</code> walks the list from the right, past the app's own proxies
					(<code>Ip.private</code>: the loopback and private ranges, or <code>{"Ip.cidrs([\"10.0.0.0/8\"])"}</code>).
					It also checks that the connection came from one of them, so a client that reaches the server
					directly can't name anyone it likes.
				</li>
				<li><code>Header(name)</code> reads one header a proxy sets, such as <code>fly-client-ip</code> or <code>cf-connecting-ip</code>.</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="flags">Feature flags</Heading>
			<p>
				A flag is defined in code: its name, what it is for, and who has it on. A rule is on or off for
				everyone (off is a kill switch), plus the users and groups it lists, plus a share of everyone else
				who is signed in. Each user falls in or out of a rollout the same way every time, by a hash of
				the flag's name and theirs, so raising the share only adds people. An environment can have a rule
				of its own.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_flag.file} source={Sources.guard_flag.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Here is who has it on, as <code>Flags.is_on</code> answered when this page was built. A user's
				bucket, 0 to 99, is that hash (<code>Flags.bucket</code>): a rollout of 25 takes those below 25.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<GuardFlags />
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				ada is listed and bea is in staff, so they have it whatever their bucket. jo and max fall in the
				quarter; cy, dee and kai don't. A visitor who isn't signed in has no id to be counted by, so a
				rollout reaches them only at 100. The environment is <code>APP_ENV</code> if it is set, or else
				<code>dev</code> when the server was started with <code>--dev</code>, or else
				<code>production</code>.
			</p>

			<h3>Changed while the server runs</h3>
			<p>
				What the code says is the default. An admin page can set a rule in its place, until it is reset.
				The server keeps that override in its memory (<a href={Paths.platform_kv}>Kv</a>), so a restart
				brings back the code's rules. An app with a database can keep overrides across restarts and
				deploys: list the package's plugin, <code>{"FlagStore.plugin({})"}</code>, and each override is
				also a row of its table (<code>guard_flags</code>, for Postgres, SQLite and Turso), read back as
				the server starts. The endpoint that sets a rule is for admins only: in the example,
				<code>Middlewares.admins</code> answers anyone else <code>403</code>.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_overrides.file} source={Sources.guard_overrides.rocx} />
			<p>
				On the server, <code>{"FlagStore.is_on!(flag, { user, groups })"}</code> answers in an endpoint,
				and <code>Guard.feature(flag, who)</code> keeps a route from anyone who doesn't have the flag on:
				they get a 404, as if it weren't there.
			</p>

			<h3>In a page</h3>
			<p>
				The server decides the flags once for the visitor, in a GET endpoint the layout reads, and a
				<code>{"<Flagged>"}</code> shows its children only when its flag is on. The server renders the
				page with the flags and puts them in it, so the browser hydrates with the same values, without
				asking again.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_visitor.file} source={Sources.guard_visitor.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.guard_layout.file} source={Sources.guard_layout.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.guard_page.file} source={Sources.guard_page.rocx} />
			<p>
				This site has no server to ask, so the example below decides the flags where the page is
				rendered, when the site is built. ada is listed for the new checkout; cy isn't, and isn't in the
				quarter the rollout reaches:
			</p>
			<div class="guard-checkouts">
				<GuardCheckout who={{ user: "ada", groups: [] }} />
				<GuardCheckout who={{ user: "cy", groups: [] }} />
			</div>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_checkout.file} source={Sources.guard_checkout.rocx} />
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>{"<Flagged>"} prop</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>name : Str</code></td><td>the flag's name</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>fallback : Html</code></td><td>what shows when the flag is off: nothing, unless given</td></tr>
						<tr><td>children</td><td>what shows when the flag is on</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>{"<FlagsContext value={…}>"}</code> is a <a href={"${Paths.components}#contexts"}>context</a>
				that provides the flags to every <code>{"<Flagged>"}</code> under it; the file that writes it needs
				<code>import pf.Context</code>. Where nothing provides them, every flag is off.
				<code>Flags.enabled(values, name)</code> reads one flag, where an <code>if</code> reads better.
			</p>

			<Heading id="guards">Other guards</Heading>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Guard</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Guard.security_headers(Security.defaults)</code></td><td>adds the headers below to every answer, but not those it sets itself</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Guard.body_limit(bytes)</code></td><td>refuses a larger body, <code>413</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Guard.methods([\"GET\", \"POST\"])"}</code></td><td>refuses any other method, <code>405</code>, with <code>Allow</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Guard.hosts([\"example.com\", \"*.example.com\"])"}</code></td><td>refuses any other <code>Host</code>, <code>400</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Guard.ips({ allow, deny, trust })"}</code></td><td>refuses an address in <code>deny</code>, or not in <code>allow</code> when it lists any, <code>403</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Guard.maintenance({ flag, message, retry_after_s, except })"}</code></td><td>while the flag is on, answers <code>503</code> with <code>Retry-After</code> and a small page, except under the paths in <code>except</code> (<code>/admin</code>, so you can turn it off again)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Guard.feature(flag, who)</code></td><td>answers <code>404</code> to anyone who doesn't have the flag on</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Guard.under(\"/api\", guard)"}</code></td><td>runs <code>guard</code> for paths under <code>/api</code> only</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>Security.defaults</code> adds these headers to every answer, as <code>Security.headers</code>
				wrote them when this page was built:
			</p>
			<Code title="Security.headers(Security.defaults, { dev: Bool.False })" source={GuardHeaders.text} plain numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The Content-Security-Policy fits a Roc Solid app. Scripts and WebAssembly come from the site's
				own origin: a page needs <code>'wasm-unsafe-eval'</code> to start its wasm under a CSP. Styles may
				be inline too, as components write <code>{"style=\"…\""}</code>, and calls go to the site's own
				server. <code>Security.directive</code> changes one directive (to allow images from another host,
				say). Under <code>--dev</code> there is no <code>Strict-Transport-Security</code>, and the CSP only
				reports: while you work, a violation shows in the console instead of breaking the page. The dev
				server's reload script is a file of the site's own (<code>/__reload.js</code>), so it is no
				violation.
			</p>

			<Heading id="modules">The modules</Heading>
			<p>
				The logic is plain Roc, on no platform, so the server, the browser and a test decide alike.
				<code>roc test packages/guard/main.roc</code> runs its <code>expect</code>s.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>RateLimit</code></td><td>a limit: <code>per_second</code>, <code>per_minute</code>, <code>per_hour</code>, <code>per(n, window_ms)</code>, <code>.sliding()</code>; <code>fresh</code>, <code>check(state, now)</code>, <code>idle</code>, <code>headers</code>, <code>explain</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Tally</code></td><td>many clients' states by key, bounded: <code>Tally.hit(tally, limit, key, now, room)</code>; <code>to_str</code>, <code>from_str</code>, <code>shard</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>ClientIp</code></td><td><code>ClientIp.of(trust, peer, headers)</code>, by <code>Direct</code>, <code>Header(name)</code>, <code>Hops(n)</code> or <code>Proxies(cidrs)</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Ip</code></td><td>IPv4 and IPv6 addresses and ranges: <code>parse</code>, <code>to_str</code>, <code>cidr</code>, <code>cidrs</code>, <code>within</code>, <code>within_any</code>, <code>key</code>, <code>private</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Flag</code></td><td>a flag in code: <code>Flag.define(name, about, rule)</code>, <code>.in_env(env, rule)</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Flags</code></td><td>rules (<code>on</code>, <code>off</code>, <code>rollout(percent)</code>, <code>only(users, groups)</code>), <code>is_on</code>, <code>values</code>, <code>enabled</code>, <code>set</code> and <code>reset</code> for overrides, <code>describe</code> for an admin page</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Security</code></td><td>security headers: <code>defaults</code>, <code>directives</code>, <code>directive</code>, <code>csp</code>, <code>headers</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Hash</code></td><td>a small, stable hash of text, the same on every machine: <code>of</code>, and <code>bucket</code>, which puts a user in or out of a rollout and a client in a tally</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Checks</code></td><td>checks on a request: <code>methods</code>, <code>hosts</code>, <code>body_size</code>, <code>ips</code>, <code>maintenance</code></td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				The middleware is the package's server half: <code>Guard</code> holds the guards,
				<code>FlagStore</code> holds the flags while the server runs, and <code>Kept</code> keeps the rate
				limits' tallies. Only the server compiles these three.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_middleware_api.file} source={Sources.guard_middleware_api.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<Code title={Sources.guard_flagstore_api.file} source={Sources.guard_flagstore_api.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The <a href="https://github.com/Roc-Solid/rocx/blob/master/packages/guard/README.md">package's
				README</a> has the whole API in one place.
			</p>

			<Heading id="status">Tested, and limits</Heading>
			<p>
				Every module of the package is tested by its <code>expect</code>s. The middleware is tested in
				<code>packages/guard/example/</code>, which builds, runs and is asked over HTTP. Its login limit
				answers <code>429</code> on the sixth try, with the headers above, and its admin endpoint answers
				<code>403</code> to anyone but an admin. Its flags' overrides turn its feature route and its
				maintenance mode on and off while it runs, and outlive a restart in its SQLite file.
			</p>
			<Note>
				The server reads a request's body, up to 16 MiB (or its <code>--max-body</code>), before any
				middleware runs, so <code>Guard.body_limit</code> refuses a large body after it has arrived, not
				before.
			</Note>
		</NoHydration>
	</>

<style>
	.guard-checkouts {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, max(14rem, calc((100% - var(--space-3)) / 2))), 1fr));
		gap: var(--space-3);
		margin: var(--space-4) 0;
	}

	.guard-checkouts p {
		margin: 0;
		padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4);
		border-radius: var(--radius);
		background: var(--bg-raised);
	}
</style>
